 S~S Style and Content Guide TSC LLC uses when writing metadata documentation

------------------------------------------

    Basic guidelines:

        Write a statement, using the form:  <who> <what> <when> <where> <how>   let's say "<WWWWH>"

            Each statement topic will be given a value by the reader if a value is not stated.

              Example: TSC LLC publishes SimpleSal content periodically on GitHub.
                <how> is assumed because without the assumed internet, GitHub might not work.

              Example: modified the interrupt handler initialization to fix Bug #1199
                <who> is the developer, <when><where><how> matters if not "today with an editor".
                <what> clearly must uniquely state specific information about a specific task.

        The answer to "Why?" is in the project documentation somewhere.

        Use spaces and indentation to show relationships between a statement and further discussion.

        Use simple graphics to separate sections within a discussion about a statement.

                =====
                statement or complete thought without sections
                =====
                statement or complete thought with sections
                ---------
                section about statement
                ---------
                section about statement
                ---------
                =====

------------------------------------------

  S~S header tags

    Place unique character strings at the top-level description in a statement or section.
    This allows file system tools to find and display organized metadata in the files.

    Outside the repo, in the developer's workspace, the human metadata content is a text database,
    searchable using simple applications.  For example, the format suggests topical sections the
    developer writes might start with a searchable tag; prepend "S~S" in front of all these lines;
    a file system tool that searches for strings in text files shows all the files and lines.

